    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `CookieJar.filter_cookies`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `filter_cookies` function in the `aiohttp.cookiejar` module is responsible for filtering and returning cookies that are applicable to a given request URL. It takes a single parameter, `request_url`, which is expected to be an instance of `yarl.URL`. If the input is not a `yarl.URL`, the function issues a deprecation warning and converts it to one. The function initializes a `SimpleCookie` or `BaseCookie` object based on the `_quote_cookie` attribute. It then checks if there are any cookies stored; if not, it returns the empty cookie object. If there are cookies, it performs expiration checks and removes any expired ones. It determines whether the request URL is secure and, if not, checks against a list of secure origins. The function then filters cookies based on domain and path matching, ensuring that only relevant cookies are included. It handles edge cases such as cookies with trailing slashes and secure flags. The function relies on helper functions like `is_ip_address` and methods like `_do_expiration`. It does not raise exceptions but may return an empty cookie object if no applicable cookies are found. The function has side effects, including updating the `_morsel_cache` with new `Morsel` objects for efficient future lookups.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transfo